{
  "title": "ROB 210 Module 3: PID Tuning for the Marlin Rover",
  "body": "This module introduces feedback control using the Marlin, a small four-wheeled teaching rover. Students will compare open-loop control, where motor commands are fixed regardless of external changes, with closed-loop control, which uses wheel encoders to measure distance and correct motor commands based on the error between the setpoint and measured value. The core task is to tune a Proportional-Integral-Derivative (PID) controller to drive the rover exactly one metre. Students will begin by tuning the proportional gain (Kp) until oscillation occurs, then halving it. Next, they will add an integral term (Ki) to eliminate steady-state error caused by static friction, being mindful of potential overshoot and integral windup. Finally, they will adjust the derivative term (Kd) to damp motion and reduce overshoot, while noting its sensitivity to encoder noise. Deliverables include a tuning table, a distance-versus-time plot, and a written explanation of the effects of each gain.",
  "citations": [1, 2, 3, 4]
}

{
  "title": "Draft: ROB 210 Module 3 - PID Tuning Guide (Instructor Notes)",
  "body": "This module introduces feedback control using the Marlin, a small four-wheeled teaching rover. Students will contrast open-loop control, where the rover drives at a fixed power setting regardless of external changes, with closed-loop control, which uses wheel encoders to measure distance and correct motor commands in real-time. The core concept is the error, e(t), defined as the difference between the setpoint and the measured value. The lab activity requires students to tune a PID controller to achieve precise one-meter travel. The procedure begins with a proportional-only approach: set Ki and Kd to zero, then increase Kp until the rover oscillates, then halve that value. Next, students incrementally add Ki to eliminate steady-state error, noting that while it removes the remaining distance error, it can cause overshoot and integral windup if the motor saturates. Finally, Kd is introduced to damp motion and reduce overshoot, with a specific threshold of 5 cm overshoot triggering its addition. Students must record overshoot, settling time, and final error for each configuration in their lab notebooks. The final report must include a tuning table, a distance-versus-time plot for the optimal gains, and a paragraph explaining the specific effect of each gain term.",
  "citations": [1, 2, 3, 4]
}

{
  "title": "ROB 210 Module 3: PID Controller Tuning FAQs",
  "body": "Q: What is the difference between open-loop and closed-loop control for the Marlin rover? A: In open-loop control, the rover drives at a set power for a set time without measuring the result, so factors like battery voltage or floor friction can change the distance. In closed-loop control, a wheel encoder measures the distance, and the controller adjusts the motor command based on the difference between the desired distance (setpoint) and the measured value (error).",
  "citations": [1]
}
